Sample Input/Output
Input: 
IRFRAUD-8254, 4381629

Output

This ticket concerns AppTastic Tech (4381629), who also own publisher account ID 3072943. They are also linked to AppTrust (2297391). They are based in Poland, and the primary business model of these accounts is as a mobile app install network. 

Account 4381629 joined the platform in June 2023. They were accepted to the marketplace at the time, but are currently not admitted in the marketplace. They have earned over $168K from our platform since then, and over $13K in the last month. They have 50 active relationships on the platform, among which are TikTok and Binance.

They have been reported for fraud concerns by Binance and TikTok. Binance expressed suspicion that their traffic and placements were so similar to those of AppTrust and that they were unable to present any detailed click reports, but the ticket was resolved without much follow-up. Our internal monitoring flagged them for click spamming for TikTok, but TikTok insisted on keeping them anyway. 

The PCA Continuous Monitoring system has flagged both AppTastic and AppTrust consistently at the critical level for at least the past year, with average scores around 90 for each. This has been largely due to:
Click spamming: these accounts have conversion rates well below 0.01%, which is extremely low in comparison to other publisher accounts
Click Stuffing: over 80% of clicks we have seen from these accounts in the past year have anomalies in the request headers that are indicative of bot or otherwise invalid traffic
High user entropy: we disproportionately see many clicks coming repeatedly from the same types of devices, indicating some kind of click farm or bot traffic

The current ticket from Helium alleges that AppTastic and other publishers are creating spoofed app orders using an SDK loophole to create fake events. They cite as supporting evidence that the click-to-action times are extremely short and that IPs are all unique. PCA is not flagging the actions as resulting from injected clicks, though, and unique IPs are not necessarily a concern. However, given the high fraud risk and historical complaints associated with these partners, we should assume that there is a high likelihood of suspicious activity.

Next steps:

Place a qualified-to-pay hold on the affected partners
Confirm the short click-to-action times
Ask the brand for more detail on the SDK loophole and any internal evidence they have that the actions were spoofed

If the allegations can be confirmed, then we should consider removing AppTastic from the platform as a whole and both removing AppTrust from the marketplace and the platform as a whole.
